    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Bounds: the Cauchy estimate\n\nIf $|f| \\le M$ on $|\\zeta - z_0| = R$, then\n\n$$|f^{(n)}(z_0)| \\le \\frac{n!\\,M}{R^n}.$$\n\nThis is the ML inequality applied to the formula for derivatives.\n\n**Liouville's theorem** falls out: a bounded entire function ($f$ holomorphic on all of $\\mathbb{C}$, $|f| \\le M$ globally) has $|f^{(n)}(z_0)| \\le n!\\,M / R^n$ for arbitrarily large $R$, so $f^{(n)}(z_0) = 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$. Thus $f$ is constant.\n\n**Fundamental theorem of algebra** is a one-line corollary: if $p(z)$ is a non-constant polynomial with no zeros, $1/p$ would be entire and bounded — by Liouville, constant. Contradiction.\n\n## Maximum modulus principle\n\nIf $f$ is holomorphic on a connected open set $\\Omega$ and $|f|$ has a *local maximum* in $\\Omega$, then $f$ is constant. Equivalently: **on a bounded domain, $|f|$ attains its maximum on the boundary**.\n\nGeometrically: a holomorphic function can't have an interior peak in $|f|$ unless it's flat. This is a consequence of Cauchy's integral formula too — average of $f$ around a small circle equals the centre value, which forces no interior local max.\n\nDemonstrate by sampling $|f(z)|$ for $f(z) = z^2 + 1$ on a disc:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "ax_draw3d(\n  explicit(cabs((x + y*%i)^2 + 1), x, -1, 1, y, -1, 1),\n  title=\"|z² + 1| on the unit disc — maxes on the boundary\",\n  xlabel=\"Re z\", ylabel=\"Im z\", zlabel=\"|f(z)|\",\n  colorscale=\"Viridis\"\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 8,
      "outputs": [
