    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The maxima of $|z^2 + 1|$ over the closed unit disc both sit on the boundary circle (at $z = \\pm 1$, where $|f| = 2$). The interior is strictly lower.\n\n## Why the boundary determines the interior\n\nThis is the deepest difference from real analysis. A real $C^\\infty$ function on $[a, b]$ is *not* determined by its boundary values $f(a)$, $f(b)$ — there are infinitely many smooth functions with any chosen pair. A holomorphic function on a disc *is* determined by boundary values via Cauchy's formula.\n\nEquivalently: holomorphic functions have **far less freedom** than real smooth ones. They're solutions of a $\\bar\\partial$-PDE, which is severely restrictive.\n\n## Putting it together\n\nWe've covered:\n- Holomorphic = complex differentiable in all directions ⇔ Cauchy-Riemann.\n- Holomorphic on a simply connected domain ⇒ contour integrals are zero (Cauchy's theorem).\n- Cauchy's integral formula reads off interior values from the boundary.\n- ⇒ holomorphic ⇒ $C^\\infty$ ⇒ analytic.\n- ⇒ maximum modulus, Liouville, FTA.\n\nThe next notebook tackles **isolated singularities**: poles, residues, and the residue theorem — the toolkit that unlocks classical real-integral evaluations.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}